1
00:00:01,068 --> 00:00:08,057
Welcome to Africa episode eight of eight. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:08,711 --> 00:00:23,723
Four island countries finish our Africa route: Madagascar, Comoros, Mauritius
and Seychelles. The three smaller countries use labelled supplemental views,
starting with a regional locator before each close-up.

3
00:00:24,379 --> 00:00:27,653
Let's bring Madagascar and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:29,912 --> 00:00:42,880
Madagascar is the large island east of Mozambique, separated from mainland
Africa by the Mozambique Channel. The smaller Indian Ocean island countries
lie around and beyond it.

5
00:00:43,536 --> 00:00:55,343
The rainforests of the Atsinanana protect habitats for lemurs and many other
species unique to Madagascar. Long isolation helped produce an extraordinary
living world unlike the mainland’s.

6
00:00:56,004 --> 00:01:07,301
Picture lemurs on the great island beside Africa. Madagascar is the large
anchor; use it to locate Comoros northwest, Mauritius east and Seychelles
farther north.

7
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:13,281
Let's locate Comoros on a labelled supplemental map, then move in for a closer
view.

8
00:01:16,508 --> 00:01:30,672
Comoros lies between northern Mozambique and northern Madagascar. This
labelled supplemental view shows the Union of the Comoros islands of Grande
Comore, Mohéli and Anjouan.

9
00:01:31,337 --> 00:01:41,484
Mount Karthala on Grande Comore is an active volcano. The islands’ volcanic
origin gives us a vivid way to remember this small country in the northern
Mozambique Channel.

10
00:01:42,137 --> 00:01:56,812
Picture a volcano northwest of Madagascar. Nearby Mayotte is administered by
France and claimed by Comoros; it is not counted as an additional country or
shown as an administered Union island here.

11
00:01:57,497 --> 00:02:04,869
Let's locate Mauritius on a labelled supplemental map, then move in for a
closer view.

12
00:02:07,101 --> 00:02:17,376
Mauritius lies east of Madagascar in the Indian Ocean. The main island is
small, so we move from a regional locator into a labelled supplemental view.

13
00:02:18,026 --> 00:02:29,926
Aapravasi Ghat in Port Louis preserves the remains of an immigration depot
associated with indentured labour. Many people arrived from India to work in
the island’s sugar industry.

14
00:02:30,586 --> 00:02:43,171
Picture an arrival depot on a small island east of Madagascar. Mauritius is
south of Seychelles; nearby Réunion is French territory and is not another
country in this series.

15
00:02:43,846 --> 00:02:50,580
Let's locate Seychelles on a labelled supplemental map, then move in for a
closer view.

16
00:02:53,383 --> 00:03:05,620
Seychelles is an Indian Ocean archipelago northeast of Madagascar and north of
Mauritius. This close view centres on Mahé, one of its main granitic islands.

17
00:03:06,283 --> 00:03:18,903
Farther southwest in the same country, Aldabra Atoll shelters giant tortoises
around a shallow lagoon. Seychelles includes widely separated island groups,
not only the islands near Mahé.

18
00:03:19,555 --> 00:03:31,838
Picture giant tortoises across a scattered island country. Keep Seychelles
north of Mauritius, and remember that the Mahé close-up does not show the
distant Aldabra group.

19
00:03:35,449 --> 00:03:53,557
Madagascar is the large island beside Mozambique. Comoros lies northwest of
its northern end, Mauritius farther east, and Seychelles farther north.
Remember lemurs, a volcano, the arrival depot and giant tortoises.

20
00:03:54,220 --> 00:04:14,092
Across eight episodes we have covered fifty-four countries once each, plus
Western Sahara as a disputed-territory stop. Somaliland was shown within
Somalia. Four missing island countries used supplemental maps with real
geographic positions and relative scale.

21
00:04:14,751 --> 00:04:29,763
Rebuild the eight regional groups using their locations and facts. These are
teaching groups, not an official regional classification; territories and map
targets do not add extra countries to the count.
